 **Parameters:**
-*   **Initial Viewpoints:** Uniformly seeded longitudes $\{45^\circ, 135^\circ, 225^\circ, 315^\circ\}$ and latitudes $\pm 45^\circ$ [S2].
-*   **Iterations:** 20 iterations of gradient descent [S2].
-*   **Camera Model:** Camera on a sphere enclosing the volume, facing the center [S2].
+*   **Image Resolution ($H \times W$):** Determines the number of pixels $N$ in the entropy calculation [S2].
+*   **Number of Iterations:** Typically 20 iterations are used for gradient descent/ascent [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Learning Rate ($\eta$):** Not explicitly specified in sources, but required for gradient descent [S3, G, unofficial].
+*   **Initial Viewpoints:** Uniformly seeded positions, e.g., longitudes $\{45^\circ, 135^\circ, 225^\circ, 315^\circ\}$ and latitudes $\pm 45^\circ$ [S3, unofficial].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Local Minima:** The optimization is sensitive to initial views and may converge to local minima [S2]. Multiple parallel runs with different seeds are recommended [S2].
-*   **Zero Alpha:** If the volume is empty or fully transparent from a viewpoint, the alpha channel sum is zero, leading to division by zero in normalization [G]. Guard against this by returning 0 entropy or skipping the step.
-*   **Precision:** Rounding errors may cause slight differences between adjoint and forward methods, but they compute the same gradients theoretically [S2].
+*   **Local Minima:** The optimization process is dependent on the initial view and may converge to local minima [S3, unofficial]. Running multiple optimizations from different initial seeds is recommended [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Zero Alpha:** If the alpha channel is all zeros, the probability calculation involves division by zero. This should be handled by returning zero entropy or skipping the step [G].
+*   **Precision:** Rounding errors may cause slight differences between adjoint and forward methods, but they compute essentially the same gradients [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Best Viewpoint Selection via Visibility Entropy](1367-best-viewpoint-selection-via-visibility-entropy.md)
-Uses a probabilistic information channel model where visibility is normalized, focusing on geometric information distribution rather than raw opacity. Choose when robustness to discretization is required.
+Best Viewpoint Selection via Visibility Entropy (VE) is a method for selecting representative camera viewpoints in volume visualization by maximizing the uniformity of the visibility distribution across the volume's voxels. It models the relationship between viewpoints and volume data as an information channel $V \rightarrow Z$, where visibility is treated as a normalized contribution to the final image rather than raw opacity accumulation. The measure is robust to any type of discretisation or resolution of the volume data set, and behaves similarly for polygonal data.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The cost is dominated by the volume rendering pass and the backward pass [S2]. On an NVIDIA RTX 2070 (Intel Xeon 8x@3.60Ghz CPU, 64GB RAM, Windows 10, CUDA 11.1), a single forward/backward pass takes 14.6 ms / 9.5 ms respectively, totaling 24.1 ms for 20 iterations [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The adjoint method requires storing the computation graph or intermediate variables for the backward pass, which can be memory-intensive [S2]. Forward-immediate stores forward variables for reuse, while forward-delayed re-traces the volume, requiring more time but potentially less memory than the adjoint method in some contexts [S2].
-*   **Performance:** Forward-immediate is slightly faster than the adjoint method (21.3 ms vs 24.1 ms total for 20 iterations in one comparison) [S2]. Forward-delayed is approximately 30% slower due to re-tracing [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Differentiable, allowing gradient-based optimization. Can find viewpoints with high visual information (entropy) more efficiently than uniform sampling [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to initialization (local minima). Computationally expensive compared to non-differentiable sampling if many views are needed [S2].
-*   **When to Use:** When a single or few optimal viewpoints are needed for visualization or analysis, and computational resources allow for gradient-based optimization [G].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time performance is critical, or when the volume is very large and rendering is too slow for many iterations [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The optimization requires multiple forward and backward passes through the volume renderer. For a single iteration, the adjoint method takes approximately 9.5 ms and the forward method takes 14.6 ms on an NVIDIA RTX 2070 with an Intel Xeon 8x@3.60Ghz CPU [S3, unofficial]. With 20 iterations, the total time is approximately 24.1 ms for the adjoint method [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The adjoint method requires storing intermediate values for the backward pass, which can be memory-intensive for large volumes [S3, unofficial]. The forward method (forward-delayed) requires re-tracing the volume, which is slower but may have different memory characteristics [S3, unofficial]. Forward-immediate stores forward variables, taking slightly less time than adjoint but potentially more memory [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** The method converges to viewpoints with high visual entropy, comparable to the best view from 256 uniformly sampled views [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Differentiable, allowing gradient-based optimization. Efficient compared to exhaustive sampling.
+*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to initial conditions (local minima). Requires a differentiable volume renderer.
+*   **When to use:** When a single or few representative viewpoints are needed for volume visualization, transfer function reconstruction, or multi-view initialization [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When the volume has very low opacity or uniform density, leading to low entropy gradients. When exhaustive sampling is feasible and sufficient.
 
 ## Background
-The method was proposed by Ji et al. [19] and applied to volume rendering optimization in the context of Differentiable Direct Volume Rendering (DiffDVR) [S2]. It is used to select the best sampled view for volume visualization by maximizing visual entropy [S2]. The approach leverages Automatic Differentiation (AD) to optimize camera parameters (longitude and latitude) [S2].
+The method was proposed by Ji et al. [19] (cited in [S2][S3, unofficial]) and implemented in the context of Differentiable Direct Volume Rendering (DiffDVR) [S2][S3, unofficial]. It is used to optimize camera parameters (longitude and latitude) to maximize a cost function, specifically opacity entropy [S3, unofficial]. The approach leverages automatic differentiation (AD) to compute gradients of the entropy metric with respect to camera parameters [S2][S3, unofficial]. It has been applied to datasets such as CT scans of human teeth, jetstream simulations, C60 molecule potential fields, and smoke plumes [S3, unofficial].
 
